| # A `.ONESHELL` script can read its own text back into a variable, and a command
| # substitution takes the trailing NEWLINES off what it captured and nothing
| # else. So the blanks the captured line ended on are in the value, and a build
| # that tidied the end of that line answers a different string.
| #
| # What the case records is the exit status alone, which is why the comparison is
| # the last line: `.ONESHELL` arms no `-e`, so a script whose capture came out
| # short runs to its end all the same and touches the file — and reports the
| # failure of the test it ended on. GNU Make 4.4.1 reports success.
| .ONESHELL:
| SP = $(subst x, ,x)
| all:
| 	v=$$(cat <<'E'
| 	a$(SP)
| 	E
| 	)
| 	touch out
| 	[ "$$v" = "a$(SP)" ]
file out touched 0
